{"command": "cd /testbed && sed -n '60,300p' specs/001-transactions/spec.md", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "**Why this priority**: Tag-based invalidation and correct LRU behavior are the\ndistinctive guarantees that justify the feature; they build directly on P1.\n\n**Independent Test**: Fill a small cache to force eviction, call invalidate on\na tag, and verify the returned count, that only matching keys were removed,\nthat evicted keys are gone, and that re-putting an evicted key behaves as a\nfresh entry.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** three live entries, two carrying tag \"a\", **When** invalidate(\"a\")\nis called, **Then** it returns 2 and only those two keys are missing.\n2. **Given** a key stored with tags (\"a\",\"b\"), **When** put(key, new_value,\ntags=(\"c\",)) is called, **Then** invalidate(\"a\") and invalidate(\"b\") each\nreturn 0 and invalidate(\"c\") returns 1.\n3. **Given** a cache of maxsize 2 with entries k1 (tag \"old\") and k2, **When**\nput(k3, v3, tags=(\"old\")) evicts k1, **Then** len(cache) is 2, invalidate\n(\"old\") returns 1 (removing only k3), and get(k1) raises KeyError.\n4. **Given** entries k1, k2, k3 in a maxsize-3 cache, **When** get(k1) is\ncalled and then put(k4, v4) evicts the least-recently-used entry, **Then** k2\n(the LRU) is evicted, k1 is still present, and get(k2) raises KeyError.\n5. **Given** a key evicted by LRU while carrying tag \"t\", **When** the same key\nis put again with tag \"t\", **Then** invalidate(\"t\") counts exactly the live\nentries carrying \"t\"; the prior evicted membership is not counted or removed.\n\n---\n\n### User Story 3 - Safe configuration and atomic replacement (Priority: P3)\n\nA user configures the cache with a maximum size and can rely that invalid input\nnever corrupts state: maxsize must be a positive integer (bool is rejected),\nand a failed put (invalid tags, or a value that cannot be copied) leaves the\npreviously stored value, its tags, and its recency position unchanged. Every\nstored value costs exactly one capacity slot regardless of its size or shape.\n\n**Why this priority**: Defensive guarantees that prevent silent corruption;\nimportant, but they protect the P1/P2 flows rather than adding new capability.\n\n**Independent Test**: Attempt to construct TaggedCache with 0, -1, 2.5, \"5\",\nTrue, False; attempt put with a bare string as tags, with a non-string tag\nentry, and with an uncopyable value; verify each raises the documented error\nand the prior state is intact.\n\n**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n\n1. **Given** maxsize values 0, -1, 2.5, \"5\", True, False, **When**\nTaggedCache(maxsize) is called, **Then** a ValueError is raised (True and\nFalse are rejected even though they are integers).\n2. **Given** a cache with key k stored under tag \"a\", **When** put(k, v,\ntags=\"a\") (bare string) is called, **Then** ValueError is raised and get(k)\nstill returns the old value with tag \"a\" still active (invalidate(\"a\") would\nstill return 1).\n3. **Given** a cache with key k stored, **When** put(k, v, tags=(\"a\", 1)) is\ncalled, **Then** ValueError is raised and the old value/tags/recency are\nunchanged.\n4. **Given** a cache with key k stored, **When** put(k, value, tags) is called\nwith a value that cannot be deep-copied, **Then** an error is raised and the\nold value, tags, and recency position are unchanged.\n5. **Given** a cache of maxsize 2, **When** two entries (large nested objects\nand a small int) are stored, **Then** both fit and a third put evicts the LRU\nentry; size never affects slot cost.\n\n---\n\n### Edge Cases\n\n- Tags given as a finite iterable (list, tuple, generator, set) of strings are\n  accepted; duplicate tags are deduplicated, so invalidate counts each live\n  entry once.\n- An empty tags iterable (including the default) stores the entry with no tag\n  memberships.\n- A bare string passed as tags is rejected (a string is an iterable of\n  characters, but it is not a valid tag collection).\n- A tag entry that is not a string is rejected.\n- Replacing an existing key with new tags removes the old tag memberships\n  before the new ones take effect (no transient double membership observable\n  through invalidate).\n- LRU eviction of a tagged entry removes its tag memberships so later reuse of\n  the same key starts clean.\n- get on a hit refreshes recency; get on a miss raises KeyError and changes\n  nothing.\n- len(cache) always equals the number of live entries (never exceeds maxsize,\n  never counts evicted or invalidated entries).\n- Keys are hashable; unhashable keys raise TypeError on put (standard Python\n  hashing contract).\n\n## Requirements *(mandatory)*\n\n### Functional Requirements\n\n**R01 - Storage and retrieval**\n\n- **FR-001**: TaggedCache MUST be constructible with a single required\n  maxsize argument.\n- **FR-002**: put(key, value, tags=()) MUST insert a new entry or replace an\n  existing one for the same key; keys MUST be hashable.\n- **FR-003**: get(key) MUST return a detached deep copy of the stored value;\n  mutating the returned object (including nested members) MUST NOT affect the\n  stored value.\n- **FR-004**: get(key) MUST raise KeyError when the key is not currently\n  stored.\n- **FR-005**: Values MUST be deep-copied on input, so later mutation of the\n  caller's original object MUST NOT change the stored content.\n- **FR-006**: tags MUST be a finite iterable of strings; duplicate tags MUST\n  be deduplicated so each live entry counts once per tag.\n- **FR-007**: A bare string passed as tags MUST be rejected with ValueError,\n  as MUST any tag entry that is not a string.\n- **FR-008**: Tag validation errors MUST be raised before any existing cache\n  state (values, tags, recency) is changed.\n\n**R02 - Invalidation and LRU behavior**\n\n- **FR-009**: invalidate(tag) MUST remove exactly the currently cached entries\n  carrying that tag and MUST return the number of entries removed.\n- **FR-010**: Replacing a key via put MUST remove its old tag memberships\n  before the new tags take effect.\n- **FR-011**: LRU eviction MUST remove the evicted entry's tag memberships so\n  no stale membership affects later reuse of the same key.\n- **FR-012**: get(key) on a hit MUST refresh that entry's LRU recency.\n- **FR-013**: len(cache) MUST return the number of live entries.\n\n**R03 - Configuration and atomicity**\n\n- **FR-014**: maxsize MUST be a positive integer; bool values (True, False)\n  MUST be rejected; invalid maxsize MUST raise ValueError.\n- **FR-015**: A put that fails validation (invalid tags) or fails to copy the\n  value MUST leave the previously stored value, its tags, and its recency\n  position unchanged.\n- **FR-016**: Every stored value MUST cost exactly one capacity slot\n  regardless of object size or shape.\n\n### Key Entities *(include if feature involves data)*\n\n- **Cache entry**: A live mapping from one hashable key to one stored value\n  plus the set of tags it carries; occupies exactly one of the maxsize slots.\n- **Tag**: A string label shared by any number of live entries; used only for\n  bulk invalidation. A tag's membership set always reflects live entries only.\n- **Recency order**: The LRU ordering over live entries, updated on put (new\n  or replaced) and on get hits; determines which entry is evicted when the\n  cache is full.\n\n## Success Criteria *(mandatory)*\n\n### Measurable Outcomes\n\n- **SC-001**: 100% of documented behaviors (FR-001 through FR-016) are\n  verified by passing tests, including the public acceptance suite\n  (acceptance_public.py) and project-owned tests; zero failures at\n  convergence.\n- **SC-002**: 100% of the existing public cachetools API keeps its existing\n  behavior: existing upstream tests pass unchanged, and no existing name,\n  signature, or documented behavior is altered or removed.\n- **SC-003**: In every invalid-input scenario (bad maxsize, bare-string tags,\n  non-string tag entries, uncopyable value), state preservation is 100%\n  observable: prior value, tag memberships, and recency order are exactly as\n  before the failed call.\n- **SC-004**: Tag accounting is exact in all tested sequences: invalidate\n  returns the precise live-entry count, len equals the live-entry count, and\n  no evicted or invalidated entry is ever counted, removed, or refreshed.\n- **SC-005**: Retrieved values are fully detached in every test: mutations to\n  returned objects (including nested members) and to caller originals after\n  put have zero observable effect on stored content.\n\n## Assumptions\n\n- Target users are Python library consumers of cachetools; the feature is a\n  new additive module (cachetools.tagged) and no user-facing configuration or\n  documentation site changes are in scope.\n- \"Deep copy\" follows the standard Python deep-copy semantics for supported\n  value types; values that cannot be deep-copied raise the copy mechanism's\n  error, and the failed put leaves prior state unchanged (FR-015).\n- Validation order: maxsize is checked at construction; tags are validated\n  before value copying, so a tag error never depends on value copyability.\n- Unhashable keys raise TypeError from standard Python hashing on put; this\n  is the language contract, not a feature-specific error.\n- Recency semantics: putting a new key makes it most-recently-used; getting a\n  hit makes that entry most-recently-used. (Whether replacing an existing key\n  via put also refreshes its recency is a candidate extension, not part of\n  this baseline; see Out of Scope.)\n- The public acceptance suite (acceptance_public.py) and upstream tests are\n  the authoritative verification; project-owned tests may be added but\n  upstream tests must remain unchanged.\n\n## Out of Scope\n\n- The Claim-A candidate extension (variant put_no_recency_refresh,\n  2026-09-21): \"overwriting an existing key via put refreshes its LRU\n  recency, making it the most-recently-used entry.\" It is recorded here as a\n  deferred intent for a later phase; the baseline spec (R01-R03) does not\n  require it and does not forbid it. If a later phase adopts it, the spec and\n  this section MUST be updated in the same change.\n- Thread safety, persistence, serialization, and any non-LRU eviction\n  policies.\n- Changes to existing cachetools classes or modules.\n"}